    "evidence_text": "detection methods perform in a cross-lingual setting, as the multilingual datasets available today are relatively small, and specific to a single target , .\n\nConcerns have been raised that cross-target performance is often considerably lower than fully supervised performance .\n\nis split into a multilingual training set and into multiple test sets to evaluate zero-shot cross-lingual and cross-target transfer.\n\nTo provide a baseline, we fine-tune a multilingual Bert model  on x-stance.\n\nIn addition, multilingual Bert can generalize to a degree both cross-lingually and in a cross-target setting.\n\nCrucially, the tweets in both languages focus on the same issue (Catalan independence); given this fact they are the first truly multilingual stance detection datasets known to us.\n\nWe found that no Romansh comments were detected and that all unexpected outputs were misclassifications of German, French or Italian comments.\n\nResults for the cross-target setting are given in Table TABREF37.\n\nWhile the effect is negligible in most settings, the cross-lingual performance clearly increases when all questions are\n\nsingle-segment experiments tell us that both the comment and the question provide crucial information.\n\nBaseline Experiments ::: How Important are Spelled-Out Targets?",
